query:
Consider
\begin{align}
\frac{d}{dx}\mathbf f=
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{3-\epsilon}{x} & 0\\[4pt]
\frac{\epsilon}{x(1-x)} & \frac{\epsilon}{1-x}
\end{pmatrix}\mathbf f,\qquad 0<x<1.
\label{eq:toyA}
\end{align}
Define $f_1=x^3 g_1,\ f_2=g_2$ (i.e.\ $T(x)=\mathrm{diag}(x^3,1)$).
What is the transformed system in $\epsilon$-form?

Consider the system
\begin{align}
\frac{d}{dx}\mathbf f=
\begin{pmatrix}
\frac{2-\epsilon}{x} & 0\\[6pt]
\frac{\epsilon}{1-x} & \frac{\epsilon}{1-x}
\end{pmatrix}\mathbf f,\qquad 0<x<1,
\label{eq:toyB}
\end{align}
What is the basis change to make it in canonical form?
